-"observed_effect": "The frontier-first selector shows a concrete but bounded advantage over the prior opportunistic queue choice. Frontier-first period: six hypothesis cells closed (short-horizon reversal FALSIFIED 37263008074, cross-sectional relative strength FALSIFIED 37304873966, volatility targeting FALSIFIED 37314710995, momentum SUPPORTED 37318950814, cross-sectional momentum FALSIFIED, 20-day breakout FALSIFIED 37414038601) plus two momentum qualification sweeps (lookback 3/5/10 in 37361000967 and lookback 20/60 in 37394344201), each with a durable artifact, an independent verifier, determinism, and a green regression suite, and with no process delta left UNVERIFIED. Opportunistic period: zero closed frontier cells, one documented selection defect (37260572520's objective came from the queued receipt item rather than a frontier cell), and one execution-blocked activation (37261348530) whose cause was a denied bash tool at that layer, i.e. an execution-environment failure that must not be counted against the selector. The queue also tracks resolution correctly (R-004 RESOLVED; R-001 next, matching the frontier's own Next action). The advantage is not absolute: the cross-sectional-momentum cell (63-day lookback) is a one-parameter variant of the already-falsified CSRS cell (20-day lookback) and its falsified verdict was its own a-priori prediction, so one of six hypothesis cells was a near-duplicate. Two durable-record defects were also found: no logs/ section exists for three frontier-first activations (37304873966, 37394344201, and the cross-sectional-momentum cell, which additionally has no run id), and the only admitted positive cell (momentum) is split across LEARNING_STATE.md lines 30-32 so its status text does not render as a table row. A fifth, smaller defect is the 5-vs-7 arity mismatch in selection_basis. Net: the selector earns RETAIN on selection quality and decisiveness, while the novelty guard and the activation-record layer are the current bottlenecks.",
